# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# Template for obsidian.env — created by scripts/setup.sh, which fills the hostname.
# Copy by the script rather than by hand; the credential values are the ones a fresh
# install fills itself, in this file, on the machine that owns them.

# ─── The public name ───
# The hostname Caddy answers and takes a certificate for. Written down once, here.
WIKI_HOSTNAME=wiki.example.org

# ─── The publisher's boundary ───
# These are Anthers' publish decision, stated in the environment so the server needs no
# build-time knowledge of them. Another operator publishing their own vault states their own.
# Defaults live in src/server/services/boundary.ts and match these exactly; setting them here
# makes the running stack's boundary readable without opening the code.
PUBLISH_EXCLUDE=Internal Wiki,90-99 Agents,00-09 Metafiles
PUBLISH_INCLUDE=00-09 Metafiles/01 Tasks
PUBLISH_BASE_FILES=Anthers Tasks.base

# ─── The vault Sync pulls (obsidian-headless, the house image) ───
# The sync engine joins your Obsidian Sync account as one more device and keeps the
# vault copy current; the server reads that copy. Its posture is the publisher's —
# set here, enforced by the entrypoint on every start:
#
# - **mirror-remote** — downloads only and reverts any local change, so this machine
#   can never become a vault editor even by accident.
# - **no config syncing** — .obsidian/ never propagates here; it is not the app's.
# - **a stated exclusion belt** — 🚨 the sync-layer half of the publish boundary, and
#   both wear it. The server refuses to serve anything outside its publish boundary
#   even if this list were empty; this list is what keeps the internal material from
#   ever *arriving* on a machine whose only job is to be public. Keep it in step with
#   the boundary below: the boundary decides what the server will serve, this decides
#   what the folder will hold — and if you widen one without the other, the wider one
#   is your true boundary. It must be set (it may be empty, by your own decision).
#
# Login: the entrypoint reuses the auth token persisted in the sync-state volume first;
# OBSIDIAN_EMAIL/OBSIDIAN_PASSWORD cover a first login into a fresh state (clear them
# afterwards if you like — they do nothing once the token exists). If the account uses
# 2FA, those cannot log in — `make obsidian-login` does the interactive route once and
# the token persists from there. OBSIDIAN_E2EE_PASSWORD is the vault's end-to-end
# encryption password, the one Obsidian's own Sync settings page shows — not the
# account password; it is only requested when the entrypoint links (or creates) the
# remote vault.
OBSIDIAN_VAULT_NAME=Anthers-Wiki
OBSIDIAN_DEVICE_NAME=droplet-anthers-wiki
OBSIDIAN_CREATE_REMOTE=false
OBSIDIAN_SYNC_MODE=mirror-remote
OBSIDIAN_EXCLUDED_FOLDERS=Internal Wiki,90-99 Agents,00-09 Metafiles/00 Attachments,.obsidian,.trash,.stfolder,.stversions,.agents
OBSIDIAN_EMAIL=
OBSIDIAN_PASSWORD=
OBSIDIAN_E2EE_PASSWORD=
OBSIDIAN_AUTH_TOKEN=

# ─── Where the stack's state lives on the host ───
# setup.sh creates it. Everything the stack keeps — the vault copy, the sync engine's
# identity and index — is under this one directory, so backing it up is one cp.
OBSIDIAN_DIR=/srv/anthers-obsidian